(version 1)

# CEC 24-pin ATX module — custom design rules.
# KiCad auto-loads <board>.kicad_dru from the project dir. These back the
# netclasses in 24pin-module.kicad_pro: a netclass `track_width` is only the
# *routing default*, so DRC will NOT flag an under-width power trace on its own.
# The track_width (min ...) constraints below make DRC actually catch it.
#
# Sizing basis (see the layout guideline): the +5VSB Hub-trunk node is PSU-5VSB-
# capped at ~3A and keeps a 1.0mm track floor (Power netclass) so a too-thin trunk
# trace is caught. The sensed pass-through rails (RAIL*) carry tens of amps via
# In2.Cu (2oz) POURS and are verified by pour copper area, NOT a per-track floor
# (see the note below the Power rule).

(rule "Power min width"
	(constraint track_width (min 1.0mm))
	(condition "A.NetClass == 'Power'"))

# NO per-track width floor on the high-current rails. Their ampacity comes from
# the In2.Cu (2oz) POURS, not from individual track width, and the INA228 Kelvin
# sense taps + supplementary connections live ON the rail nets themselves (the
# INA senses across the shunt). A net-/class-based track_width floor would flag
# those legitimate thin tracks (0.2mm sense taps, 1-3mm In2.Cu links), so it is
# deliberately omitted. Verify rail current capacity from the pour copper area
# (IPC-2152) in the GUI. The HighCurrent netclass still gives 0.4mm clearance
# (below) and larger via sizing for isolation.

# No special high-current clearance rule. At <=12V, 0.2mm (Default) is electrically
# ample, and a wider rail clearance conflicts with the fine-pitch INA228 Kelvin
# sense where the rail HI/LO nets enter the IC (HI/LO sit ~40mV apart across the
# shunt, so isolating them buys nothing). The HighCurrent netclass is kept for its
# larger default via size; rails use the Default 0.2mm clearance.

# USB2.0 debug pair: keep D+/D- coupled. Full-Speed (ESP32-S3 native USB) is
# impedance-tolerant, but flag long uncoupled stretches.
(rule "USB diff pair gap"
	(constraint diff_pair_gap (min 0.1mm) (max 0.2mm))
	(condition "A.NetClass == 'USB' && B.NetClass == 'USB'"))
